---
<p class="lede">Five steps from nothing to a native executable, including one error and its fix. You need a terminal and a few minutes.</p>

<ol class="steps">
  <li>
    <h2 id="get">Get the compiler</h2>
    <p>Download the 0.4.1 archive for your machine from the <a href="https://github.com/ikhbat-org/bayan/releases">releases page</a>, unpack it and put <code>bayan</code> on <code>PATH</code>. A C compiler such as <code>zig</code> must also be on <code>PATH</code> to turn the generated C into an executable. <a href="install.html">Install Bayan</a> covers archive names, checksum and source build.</p>
<pre><code>bayan --version
bayan doctor</code></pre>
    <p><code>bayan doctor</code> checks the C compiler, zig, git and the build cache, and reports gaps and fixes.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="write">Write hello</h2>
    <p>Create <code>hello.byn</code>; files end in <code>.byn</code>, and newlines end statements, so no semicolon.</p>
    <example name="hello"/>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="build">Build it</h2>
    <p><code>bayan build</code> compiles to C, then to a native executable; <code>-o</code> names the result.</p>
<pre><code>bayan build hello.byn -o hello</code></pre>
    <p><code>--emit-c</code> shows the generated C.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="run">Run it</h2>
<pre><code>./hello</code></pre>
    <p>It prints <code>Salam, world!</code>; <code>bayan run hello.byn</code> does both at once.</p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <h2 id="error">Read an error, then fix it</h2>
    <p>Save this as <code>width.byn</code>. The second parameter carries the particle <code>until</code>, which the call leaves out.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 width(i32 start, until i32 end) =&gt; end - start

let start: i32 = 2
let end: i32 = 5
print($"{width(end, start)}")</code></pre>
<pre><code>bayan run width.byn</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5005:</span> `end` is passed without its particle `until:`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> width.byn:5:21

<span class="gut">  5 │</span> print($"{width(end, <span class="hl">start</span>)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>                     <span class="hl">^^^^^ needs `until:`</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
<span class="gut">  help:</span> add `until:` before the argument
<span class="gut">    │ 5 │</span> print($"{width(end, <span class="add">until: </span>start)}")
<span class="gut">    │   │</span>                     <span class="add">+++++++</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 1 error, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN5005</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>width.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>It names the fault (a missing particle), the place (line 5, column 21) and the fix (add <code>until:</code>). But the suggested edit keeps them in the wrong order: the particle belongs on <code>end</code>, the end of the range. Fix that and run again:</p>
    <example name="width"/>
    <div class="next-box">
      <p>Next, read how <a href="parameters.html#particles">argument particles</a> work, or run <code>bayan explain BYN5005</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </li>
</ol>
